;; proc-identity-vocabulary.wat.intueri — arc 170 closure #6.
;;
;; THE PROBLEM: `ps` shows N identical `wat` processes. An operator staring at a
;; leak or a hang cannot tell a bracket worker from a service. Each spawned
;; runtime should carry its identity on the proc line, as the EDN it already is:
;;
;;     /usr/local/bin/wat <LABEL>
;;
;; ── WHAT IS ALREADY RULED (do NOT propose changing these) ────────────────────
;;
;; · The label is a WITNESS, never a CLAIM. It DESCRIBES; it must never ROUTE.
;;   fd 3 (LIFELINE_FD) stays the sole gate for "was I spawned"
;;   (`exec_plan.rs:29-35` already rejects a `--forms-server` flag for exactly
;;   this reason). Nothing may ever parse the label.
;; · It is FIXED AT BOOT — identity, not state. Set once, immutable for life.
;; · It rides a new `identity` field on `:wat::spawn::ProcessOpts`, supplied by
;;   the caller, `Option`-typed: `:None` => NO label, today's bare `wat` line.
;; · Rendering is the substrate's uniform EDN rule, not a choice:
;;   record -> `#ns/Name {field-map}`; a no-field record renders `{}`.
;;   Consequence kept deliberately: `ps` output is readable by `edn::read`.
;;
;; ── THE SLOT TO NAME, AND IT IS A SHAPE QUESTION FIRST ───────────────────────
;;
;; Two shapes are in play. They are NOT spelling variants — they differ in who
;; owns the type and whether `ps` output is a closed set.

;; ── SHAPE 1 — the tag VARIES; each caller mints its own identity type ────────
;;     /usr/local/bin/wat #wat.brackets/Worker {:id 3}
;;     /usr/local/bin/wat #my.app/CounterSvc {}
;; The service's own type IS the tag. Maximally specific per process; but every
;; service that wants to be visible must define a record type for the purpose,
;; and `ps` output is an OPEN set (any tag may appear).

;; ── SHAPE 2 — a CLOSED set of two substrate-owned types, varying part as data ─
;;     /usr/local/bin/wat #wat.process/Bracket {:id 3}
;;     /usr/local/bin/wat #wat.process/Service {:name :my::app::CounterSvc}
;; Two types the substrate owns. No caller defines anything to be visible, and
;; `ps` output is a CLOSED set a reader can match exhaustively.
;;
;; ★ IF SHAPE 2: the carrier of `:name` is its own question. Written as a
;;   String (`{:name ":my::app::CounterSvc"}`) it is a stringly-typed name, and
;;   this substrate keeps killing those. A keyword is the candidate. Weigh it.
;;
;; ★ AND IF SHAPE 2: `:wat::process::` DOES NOT EXIST in the tree today (grep:
;;   zero hits). Minting a namespace is a real cost — weigh whether an existing
;;   one already speaks. Live neighbours: `:wat::spawn::` (ProcessOpts,
;;   ThreadOpts, PeerKind — "where and how to execute"), `:wat::program::`
;;   (Env, PeerKind — "what a running program knows about itself"),
;;   `:wat::bracket::` (the worker pool). `:wat::program::` in particular
;;   already owns "facts about this process".

;; ── THE FORMS, so the names are judged where they are READ ───────────────────

;; the field the label rides (ruled; only its TYPE-ARG name is in question)
(:wat::core::defstruct :wat::spawn::ProcessOpts
  [post-spawn-fn     <- :wat::core::Fn(wat::spawn::ProcessLaunch)->wat::core::nil
   env-fn            <- :wat::core::String
   identity          <- (:wat::core::Option :wat::core::Record)   ;; <- the slot
   max-message-bytes <- :wat::core::i64
   runner-count      <- :wat::core::i64])

;; SHAPE 2's two types, if chosen — name the TYPES, the NAMESPACE, and the FIELDS
(:wat::core::defrecord :wat::process::Bracket [id   <- :wat::core::i64])
(:wat::core::defrecord :wat::process::Service [name <- :wat::core::String])

;; ── WHAT THE READER OF `ps` ACTUALLY SEES (the real use site) ────────────────
;;
;;   $ ps -o args= -C wat
;;   /usr/local/bin/wat
;;   /usr/local/bin/wat #wat.process/Bracket {:id 0}
;;   /usr/local/bin/wat #wat.process/Bracket {:id 1}
;;   /usr/local/bin/wat #wat.process/Service {:name :my::app::CounterSvc}
;;
;; The first line is a spawn with `:None`. Judge the names as a COLUMN of these,
;; scanned by an operator under pressure — not as declarations in isolation.
;;
;; ── ALSO NAME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; · the `identity` FIELD on ProcessOpts — does it say what it is, beside
;;   `env-fn` / `post-spawn-fn` / `runner-count`? (candidates: identity, label,
;;   proc-identity, badge, …). Note it must not be confused with `user.program`
;;   on `:wat::program::Env`, which is a CHILD-computed value — a different
;;   question entirely, and conflating the two is what STOPPED the first strike.
